INT. MONASTERY WINE CELLAR - EVENING

Barrel vaults of ancient brick, sweating with damp. Rows of oaken casks stretch away into shadow like sleeping animals. One candle, stuck on a keg, throws a trembling gold circle onto the flagstones.

A wounded soldier in a torn gray greatcoat sits slumped against a cask with his boots off and a filthy bandage around his thigh. He is a STRANGER to this place, forty, unshaven, eyes sharp even in pain. His rifle lies across his lap, its muzzle pointed at the stairs.

On the stairs, a pair of old sandals descends. BROTHER AMBROSE, 70s, white-haired, in a brown habit patched at the knee, carries a bowl of broth and a loaf of black bread. He stops three steps from the bottom.

BROTHER AMBROSE
I have brought supper.

The Stranger does not answer. The rifle does not move.

BROTHER AMBROSE (CONT'D)
You may shoot me after you eat. It would be a waste to shoot me before.

STRANGER
Put it on the floor.

Ambrose sets down the bowl and bread on the bottom step. He retreats. The Stranger drags himself forward, takes the bowl, drinks, hands shaking. He tears the bread with his teeth.

BROTHER AMBROSE
There is a doctor in the village. He is not one of yours, but he is a good doctor.

STRANGER
My own doctors are dead.

BROTHER AMBROSE
All of the doctors are dead, my son. The ones left are only men.

The Stranger studies him. His shoulders sag a fraction. He sets the bowl down.

STRANGER
Lukas. My name is Lukas.

BROTHER AMBROSE
Lukas. I am Ambrose.

LUKAS
The monks, are there others?

BROTHER AMBROSE
Eleven. Upstairs. Praying for rain.

LUKAS
It will rain.

BROTHER AMBROSE
It always does. Then the walls weep.

Footsteps overhead. Boots, many pairs, ringing on flagstone. A voice barks a command in another tongue. Dust sifts from the ceiling into the candle.

Lukas lifts the rifle. Ambrose raises both palms.

BROTHER AMBROSE (CONT'D)
They are not yours.

LUKAS
They are not yours either.

Lukas turns to the cask beside him and puts a hand on the bung. Ambrose watches, grave. The boots stop above the cellar door. A latch clanks.

BROTHER AMBROSE ^
Lukas, in the name of God, put it down.

LUKAS
Ambrose, in the name of God, hide.

Ambrose does not move.

SERGEANT VOGEL (O.S.)
(from above, gruff)
Captain! The cellar door is unbarred!

The door cracks open at the top of the stairs. A thin white line of lamplight falls down the steps.

CAPTAIN ISOLDE KESSLER, 30s, black coat, officer's cap, steps through. A pistol in her gloved hand, held low. She descends, slowly, boots ringing, into the candlelight.

KESSLER
(calm, accented)
Good evening, Brother. Good evening, soldier.

LUKAS
Good evening, Captain.

KESSLER
You are bleeding on the church's floor.

LUKAS
Forgive me.

She stops on the bottom step. Looks at the bowl, the bread, the rifle. A very long silence.

KESSLER
Brother Ambrose, I came to count the casks. I find only eleven.

BROTHER AMBROSE
There were twelve, Captain.

KESSLER
And the twelfth?

BROTHER AMBROSE
The twelfth is a man.

Kessler lowers the pistol an inch. Lukas does not lower the rifle. The candle gutters. Overhead, thunder rolls.

KESSLER
Soldier, it is raining.

LUKAS
It always does.

She turns her head to the stairs and listens. Beyond the door, her men shuffle.

KESSLER
(to the cellar, quietly)
There is no one here. Resume the search upstairs.

The boots retreat. The latch closes. Kessler holsters the pistol. She sits on the step, takes off her cap, and runs her hand through her hair.

KESSLER (CONT'D)
Eat. All of you.
